00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:07.760
In this video, we'll look at what the overall process of supervised learning is like.

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:14.120
Specifically, you see the first model of this course, a linear regression model.

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:17.440
That just means fitting a straight line to your data.

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:21.840
It's probably the most widely used learning algorithm in the world today.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:27.240
And as you get familiar with linear regression, many of the concepts you see here will also

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:30.420
apply to other machine learning models.

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:34.300
That's what you'll see later in this specialization.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:38.340
Let's start with a problem that you can address using linear regression.

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:42.180
Say you want to predict the price of a house based on the size of a house.

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:45.460
This is an example we see in earlier this week.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:51.100
We're going to use a dataset on the house sizes and prices from Portland, a city in

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:52.980
the United States.

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:58.020
Here with a graph where the horizontal axis is the size of the house in square feet,

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:03.660
and the vertical axis is the price of house in thousands of dollars.

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:08.260
Let's go ahead and plot the data points for various houses in the dataset.

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:14.340
Here at each data point, each of these little crosses is a house with a size and a price

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.780
that most recently was sold for.

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:21.940
Now let's say you're a real estate agent in Portland, and you're hoping a client

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:23.620
sell her house.

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:27.700
And she's asking you, how much do you think you're going to get for this house?

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:32.420
This dataset might help you estimate the price she could get for it.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:38.180
You start by measuring the size of the house, and it turns out that her house is 1250 square

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:39.300
feet.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:42.420
How much do you think this house could sell for?

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:47.900
One thing you could do is you can build a linear regression model from this dataset.

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:53.260
Your model will fit a straight line to the data, which might look like this.

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:57.700
And based on this straight line fit to the data, you can kind of see that if a house is

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:04.540
1250 square feet, it will intersect the best fit line over here.

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:09.180
And if you trace that to the vertical axis on the left, you can see the prices maybe

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:13.100
around here, say about $220,000.

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:17.940
So this is an example of what's called a supervised learning model.

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:22.620
We call this supervised learning because you are first training a model by giving a data

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:24.580
that has the right answers.

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:28.540
Because you give the model examples of houses with both the size of the house, as well as

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:32.220
the price that the model should predict for each house.

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:38.940
Where here are the prices, that is the right answers are given for every house in the dataset.

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:43.700
This linear regression model is a particular type of supervised learning model is called

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:49.740
a regression model because it predicts numbers as the output, like prices and dollars.

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:57.820
Any supervised learning model that predicts a number such as 220,000 or 1.5 or negative

00:02:57.820 --> 00:03:03.500
33.2 is addressing what's called a regression problem.

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:07.700
So linear regression is one example of a regression model.

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:12.420
But there are other models for addressing regression problems too.

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:18.060
And we'll see some of those later in course two of this specialization.

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:23.260
And just to remind you, in contrast with the regression model, the other most common

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:28.580
type of supervised learning model is called a classification model.

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:34.580
A classification model predicts categories or discrete categories, such as predicting

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:42.340
if a picture is of a cat or a dog or if given the medical record, it has to predict

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:45.420
if a patient has a particular disease.

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:50.140
You see more about classification models later in this course as well.

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:55.660
So as a reminder about the difference between classification and regression, in classification,

00:03:55.660 --> 00:03:59.140
there are only a small number of possible outputs.

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:05.380
If your model is recognizing cats versus dogs, that's two possible outputs.

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:12.720
Or maybe you're trying to recognize any of 10 possible medical conditions in a patient.

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:19.000
So if there's a discrete finite set of possible outputs, we call it a classification problem.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:23.000
Whereas in regression, there are infinitely many possible numbers that the model could

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:24.520
output.

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:29.920
In addition to visualizing this data as a plot here on the left, there's one other

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:34.320
way of looking at the data that would be useful.

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:38.760
And that's a data table here on the right.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:44.640
If the data comprises a set of inputs, this would be the size of the house, which is this

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:46.600
column here.

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:49.240
It also has outputs.

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:55.040
You're trying to predict the price, which is this column here.

00:04:55.040 --> 00:05:02.720
Notice that the horizontal and vertical axes correspond to these two columns, the size

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:05.120
and the price.

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:15.000
And so if you have say 47 rows in this data table, then there are 47 of these lower crosses

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:22.800
on the plot of the left, each cross corresponding to one row of the table.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:31.320
For example, the first row of the table is a house with size 2104 square feet.

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:35.320
So that's around here.

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:42.360
And this house so for $400,000, which is around here.

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:49.480
So this first row of the table is plotted as this data point over here.

00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:53.920
Now let's look at some notation for describing the data.

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:58.920
This is notation that you find useful throughout your journey in machine learning.

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:04.240
As you increasingly get familiar with machine learning terminology, this would be terminology

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:08.880
they can use to talk about machine learning concepts with others as well.

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:14.960
Since a lot of this is quite standard across AI, you'll be seeing this notation multiple

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:16.920
times in the specialization.

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:21.440
So it's okay if you don't remember everything the first time through, it will naturally

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:24.960
become more familiar over time.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:33.720
The data set that you just saw, and that is used to train the model is called a training set.

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:38.660
Note that your client's house is not in this data set because it's not yet sold.

00:06:38.660 --> 00:06:41.460
So no one knows what is prices.

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:46.480
So to predict the price of your client's house, you first train your model to learn

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:48.340
from the training set.

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:53.920
And that model can then predict your client's houses price.

00:06:53.920 --> 00:07:00.840
In machine learning, the standard notation to denote the input here is lowercase x, and

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:09.480
we call this the input variable is also called a feature or an input feature.

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:15.520
For example, for the first house in your training set, x is the size of the house.

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:20.200
So x equals 2104.

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:26.820
The standard notation to denote the output variable, which you're trying to predict,

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:35.520
which is also sometimes called the target variable is lowercase y.

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:39.920
And so here, y is the price of the house.

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:44.400
And for the first training example, this is equal to 400.

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:48.120
So y equals 400.

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:52.320
So the data set has one row for each house.

00:07:52.320 --> 00:08:01.440
And in this particular training set, there are 47 rows with each row representing a different

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:04.100
training example.

00:08:04.100 --> 00:08:09.480
We're going to use lowercase m to refer to the total number of training examples.

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:13.920
And so here, m is equal to 47.

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:20.720
To indicate a single training example, we're going to use the notation parentheses x comma

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:22.220
y.

00:08:22.220 --> 00:08:34.200
So for the first training example, x comma y, this pair of numbers is 2104 comma 400.

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:37.360
Now we have a lot of different training examples.

00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:39.360
We have 47 of them, in fact.

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:45.880
So to refer to a specific training example, this will correspond to a specific row in

00:08:45.880 --> 00:08:47.600
this table on the left.

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:56.120
I'm going to use the notation x superscript in parentheses, I comma y superscript in parentheses

00:08:56.120 --> 00:08:57.120
I.

00:08:57.120 --> 00:09:06.000
This superscript tells us that this is the I training example, such as the first, second,

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:09.360
or third up to the 47th training example.

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:15.880
I here refers to a specific row in the table.

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:21.600
So for instance, here is the first example.

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:25.960
When I equals one in the training set.

00:09:25.960 --> 00:09:31.960
And so x superscript one is equal to 2104.

00:09:31.960 --> 00:09:37.200
And y superscript one is equal to 400.

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:42.400
And let's add the superscript one here as well.

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:48.240
Just a note, this superscript I in parentheses is not exponentiation.

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:53.560
So when I write this, this is not x squared.

00:09:53.560 --> 00:09:55.880
This is not x to the power of two.

00:09:55.880 --> 00:09:59.560
It just refers to the second training example.

00:09:59.560 --> 00:10:06.640
So this I is just an index in the training set and refers to row I in the table.

00:10:06.640 --> 00:10:11.600
In this video, you saw what a training set is like, as was a standard notation for

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:13.760
describing this training set.

